{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518781836,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUyOTc3NjA0ODA=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2180","origin":"github"},"title":"Make `subdatasets` make clever guesses on absent subdatasets","message":"`metadata --get-aggregates` can report the entire hierarchy of subdatasets underneath, as long as metadata was aggregated at some point (doesn't even have to have happended in one go).\n\n`subdatasets` on the other hand is limited to one level below the last actually installed dataset.\n\nWe could make it more clever and have it reveal subdatasets based on the metadata knowledge (which could be outdated, but better than no idea at all...).\n\nIf we do this, this has implications for `install/get` (at least). ATM we try to install everything along a given path in order to determine whether the actual target even exists -- this can take a while. We can easily query the metadata (very low latencyin comparison), if there is any knowledge about anything at the target path (down to the level of an individual file). If we find something, we can install with more confidence (still no guarantee). If we find nothing, we can inform the user that there likely is nothing, and can have a `--machine-obey-me` switch to ignore this bit of wisdom.\n\nWhat do you think?","files":null}]}